# This example's whole point rests on byte-exact files: items/test.jsonl is
# sha256-sealed in eval-card.json (Gate 5), and protocol.md is sha256-hashed
# into preregistration.content_hash. Without this file, a checkout on a
# machine with core.autocrlf=true (the default this repo's environment uses,
# and there is no repo-wide .gitattributes) silently rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout, which changes both files' bytes and invalidates both hashes --
# not because anyone tampered with the split, but because of how git checked
# it out. That would make the example self-tamper on a plain `git clone`.
#
# Forcing LF here means the bytes on disk always match what was hashed,
# regardless of the checking-out machine's line-ending settings.
* text=auto eol=lf
